1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,915
There's a man who kept fighting World War

2
00:00:01,997 --> 00:00:04,690
Two... until 1974.

3
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:07,115
His name was Hiroo Onoda.

4
00:00:07,556 --> 00:00:11,248
Japanese intelligence officer. Sent deep into the Philippine

5
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:13,210
jungle during World War Two.

6
00:00:13,509 --> 00:00:19,222
And then — the war ended.

7
00:00:19,663 --> 00:00:22,936
In 1945. And nobody told him.

8
00:00:23,237 --> 00:00:26,348
Planes dropped leaflets saying the war was over.

9
00:00:26,674 --> 00:00:29,112
He looked at them and thought — enemy

10
00:00:29,170 --> 00:00:31,457
propaganda. Obviously.

11
00:00:31,757 --> 00:00:33,626
He found actual newspapers.

12
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:37,307
Forgeries, he decided. People came into the jungle

13
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:40,267
to find him — he ambushed them.

14
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:43,274
Because that's what soldiers do.

15
00:00:43,573 --> 00:00:46,185
Meanwhile — the world just...

16
00:00:46,696 --> 00:00:49,564
moved on. Japan rebuilt.

17
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:51,781
Cities rose from ash.

18
00:00:51,990 --> 00:00:55,183
Babies were born, grew up, had their OWN

19
00:00:55,265 --> 00:00:55,926
babies.

20
00:00:56,226 --> 00:00:58,745
And Hiroo was still in that jungle.

21
00:00:59,256 --> 00:01:02,890
Sharpening his blade. Keeping his rifle clean.

22
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:04,447
Waiting.

23
00:01:04,746 --> 00:01:09,912
Fifty years. FIFTY. He was thirty years old

24
00:01:09,947 --> 00:01:10,772
when he went in.

25
00:01:10,888 --> 00:01:13,153
He was turning fifty-two in that jungle.

26
00:01:13,453 --> 00:01:17,435
Then in 1974 — his original commanding officer

27
00:01:17,493 --> 00:01:18,736
flew to the Philippines.

28
00:01:18,909 --> 00:01:20,406
Walked into that jungle.

29
00:01:20,731 --> 00:01:22,368
In his old uniform.

30
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:25,408
And gave Hiroo the only order he'd ever

31
00:01:25,478 --> 00:01:29,588
accept. Stand down. The war is over.

32
00:01:29,887 --> 00:01:32,581
Hiroo walked out of that jungle at fifty-two

33
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:35,553
years old. Still in his uniform.

34
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:37,642
Still carrying his weapon.

35
00:01:37,968 --> 00:01:41,033
Into a world he didn't recognize.

36
00:01:41,332 --> 00:01:43,248
And here's what wrecks me about this —

37
00:01:43,364 --> 00:01:45,941
he wasn't crazy. He wasn't delusional.

38
00:01:46,115 --> 00:01:48,646
He was the most disciplined, loyal person you

39
00:01:48,704 --> 00:01:50,109
could imagine.

40
00:01:50,409 --> 00:01:53,834
His greatest strength... was also his cage.

41
00:01:54,438 --> 00:01:56,307
He was so committed to the mission —

42
00:01:56,911 --> 00:01:58,954
he couldn't see the mission had become the

43
00:01:59,012 --> 00:02:00,115
prison.

44
00:02:00,415 --> 00:02:03,712
And honestly? I think a lot of us

45
00:02:03,747 --> 00:02:05,814
are doing the exact same thing.

46
00:02:06,256 --> 00:02:08,716
Still fighting fights that ended years ago.

47
00:02:09,042 --> 00:02:11,212
Still loyal to a version of someone —

48
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:14,254
or ourselves — that doesn't exist anymore.

49
00:02:14,555 --> 00:02:16,656
The question isn't whether you're committed.

50
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:19,942
The question is — are you guarding a

51
00:02:20,012 --> 00:02:21,985
post no one needs anymore?

